ork together on stuff and we  argue and to be dramatic and I have  really fond memories of this now because  it's a great teaching it  so basically what's putting out a  newspaper is like which is what I do  every day now in all the ways it's  really representative of what it takes  to be successful in a really meaningful  career once you graduate college the  long nights for studying the rigorous  demands that I thought almost Ron fares  at times and really helped prepare me  for what it took to be a journalist here  where you have deadlines you have  multiple stories a day you're  increasingly more competitive journalism  environment or more and more as demanded  [Music]  well I think one of the coolest things  about the DAO journalism program the  opportunity for networking with really  incredible journalists I mean while I  was there we had Naomi Shafer Riley come  in she spoke with our class talking with  her I was able to actually pitch her an  idea as a student that ended up  published in The Wall Street Journal  while I was still in Hillsdale it was  just this amazing opportunity for me to  be faced with this group of extremely  eager 18 19 and 20 year olds how eager  they were to do the work to ask the  questions that are necessary not only to  be a good journalist but to be a good  citizen to be a professional we're kind  of a career development office for  people interested in the media we work  very hard to help our students land jobs  upon graduation in fact everybody who  miners in journalism who wants a job in  journalism pretty much gets one I would  much rather hire somebody who knows  something and teach them the craft then  find someone who's only learned the  craft but doesn't know any the  journalism is a minor there right so you  get to sort of dip your toes in it he  learned some important things but at the  same time the emphasis remains on solid  scholarship and solid citizenship if you  learn the important stuff that much  easier to learn t